Subject: register VM precomputes a per-function call descriptor

What this does:
  A JS-to-JS call through the record VM tests four fields of the callee every
  time it picks the fast path: `func_kind == JS_FUNC_NORMAL`, `regvm_compiled`,
  `!is_lazy` and `regvm_all_threaded`. Those inputs settle once the function's
  bytecode and metadata are final, so the conjunction is now computed there and
  stored as a single byte, `regvm_call_ready`, and the hot classifier is one
  load and one branch instead of four loads and three compares.

  The arena size for a frame is also recomputed from scratch on every call:
  `sizeof(JSRegRun)` plus the interpreter registers (from three counts), plus
  the variable-reference table and the control-block array. All of those counts
  are likewise final, so `regvm_frame_need` stores the size for the borrow case
  (`arg_slots == 0`), and `regvm_record_alloc` is one load and one add of the
  reserved argument area instead of three multiplies.

  `regvm_set_call_desc()` refreshes both fields at the three points where a
  function becomes fully defined: the end of `js_create_function`, the non-lazy
  deserialize success path, and a successful lazy materialization. It is
  guarded by `QJS_REGVM_RECORD` because the record layout type and the
  interpreter register counts only exist in that build. A function read from a
  serialized blob keeps `regvm_all_threaded == 0` (that flag is never
  serialized), so it stays ineligible exactly as before.

  Measured (`fixed.js`, 32 iterations, i7-8700), interleaved against 0150:
  22,895,7xx,xxx -> 22,758,9xx,xxx instructions (**-0.136 G, -0.59 %**), cycles
  flat within noise. On a 20 M-iteration empty JS-to-JS call benchmark the
  per-call cost drops a uniform 12 instructions (272 -> 260, including the
  4-argument and method forms); native calls are unchanged because they build no
  record. Gate run 22,758,873,063 / 10,857,923,737; box2d checksum exact;
  diff-suite 398/0/0 mono and threaded; leak assertion clean; peak RSS 13368 KB.
--- a/quickjs.c
